Fiber Optic Sensing of Recovery Requiring a Chest Tube

Benjamin Schreyer

Introduction

We propose development of a fiber optic sensing and analysis technique for continuous chest injury monitoring via chest tube devices used to drain fluid or gas from the pleural cavity already inserted as part of a treatment procedure. Teams have long listened externally by ear, stethoscope (auscultation) [2] to such injuries like pneumothorax, even being able to determine spatial features or determine if drainage was complete [8], ahead of penetrating scans [4] to image the chest. These media both indicate distinctly spatial or distinctly temporal signals about the injury in order to monitor recovery of the patient and are minimally invasive. It is possible to resolve vibrations by rapid multiple imaging but the use of radiation in for example CT scans limits their use in patients. Auscultation suffers from the fact that, ”the lung parenchyma and chest wall act as a low-pass filter” [9].  To overcome these limitations this end we propose a research program which combines device development and testing against existing methodology which may be feasible on the scale of a year. Further testing in patients is not unrealistic. 

