Device

We plan to develop a chest tube which has an embedded fiber optic sensing element. The internal nature of the device is in contrast with sensors which are wearable [5]. Fiber optic Bragg sensing elements can be closely spaced and smaller than the walls of a typical chest tube device [1], such that the geometry of the chest tube may remain identical to those currently employed. With only internal structure changing, see Figure 1, to allow a fiber sensing element to run along the chest tube, and often parallel and against the affected cavity tissue, Figure 2. The augmented chest tube would also remain chemically and electromagnetically inert, desirable for use with traditional sensing for chest injuries. The external device for such a chest tube does become more complex, now not just requiring typical pressure related apparatus for drainage and sensing, but also an optical sensing interrogator to monitor space resolved vibrational signals generated within the patients chest.

