Evaluation

The prototype stage would be tested in animal cadavers. Chest tubes with an embedded fiber sensor would be used to collect data. Multiple fiber sensing could be explored towards triangulation of signal. Damage to chest tissue which shares qualities with human injury are first induced. Insertion of the tube may be made with active sensing. Breath-like pressures can be introduced by control of an external pump aimed at breath simulation. Sealing of the tube during some sensing will allow for comparison of signal clarity under this variation. At the same time traditional static imaging, pressure monitoring, and external stethoscopic measurement would produce data on the breath cycles. With fiber sensing and without fiber, datasets can be fed into machine learning models to compare their predictive power to assess the injury. Explainability is crucial in a medical setting so in parallel this data can be analyzed from a more traditional signal processing lens to extract views of chest tube fiber optic sensing which are repeatable and can be understood by doctors. Given that no human is trained to read chest-internal fiber optic signals, comparison by machine learning provides a less biased comparison to existing sensing. 

